In the Invite Only challenge, I will be analyzing flagged artefacts in order to transform them into usable threat intelligence.

Situation: I am given a flagged IP address and a SHA256 hash to analyze. Within the room, I also have access to a built in threat intelligence application. 

Since SHA256 hashes are all associated with files, my first step was to find the file it was connected to. 
Immediately, I copied the hash into Virus Total. It quickly returned the file name and type, which ended up being an executable, classified specifically as a WIN32 EXE.

My next goal was to find the execution parents of the hash. I found it by looking in the relations section of the VT report. I saved these hashes for later use. In the relations section, I also found the name of the file that was being dropped.

The challenge said to focus on the has of the second execution parent. After copying this hash, I found 4 different dropped files in the relations section. 

Next, I moved on to investigate the IP address. In the community section, I found the SyncRat tag, telling me that was the malware family these files came from.

Then, I had an unexpected question to answer, the original report that this information came from. I almost missed, but in the same comment where I found the tag, the original website was linked as well.

The rest of the questions came from the article, so I looked it up in my browser to learn more about the threat. After reading through the article, I gained some more background information.

The article is published by Checkpoint, talking about a malware campaign that used expired and released discord links. Attackers could send payloads that would normally be blocked by Windows detection systems.
In addition, attackers would use developed tools to steal cookies from browsers. 
To carry out malicious processes, they would use the Clickfix phishing technique, which is essentially when attackers create fake issues and have fake interface such as buttons to "fix it". Often times, it is imitating critical issues, pressuring users to act fast.

As mentioned, these attackers would use the legitimate platform called Discord to send them to illegitimate locations.

This challenge was relatively simple, which is a good sign, it means my skills are getting stronger!
